addSubject({id:'geo', topics:[{"t": "Exam skills: generalisations, maps, cartoons, case studies", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>answer the 5-part question pattern (A–E) in each section</li><li>write generalisations, read maps, graphs and cartoons</li><li>apply BOLTSSNA / BALTSSNA conventions to maps and graphs</li><li>write a case study with location, effects and strategies</li></ul></div><h3>1. How every KSSC Geography question works</h3><table><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it asks</th><th>How to answer</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>define a key term</td><td>one clear sentence + example</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>describe a pattern / distribution from a map or graph</td><td>use <b>names, directions and figures</b> from the resource</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>explain factors and processes</td><td>cause → process → result; use a diagram</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>effects on people and environment</td><td>positive AND negative; social, economic, environmental</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>evaluate strategies / case study (most marks)</td><td>name a place + date; describe ≥3 strategies; judge how well each works</td></tr></table><h3>2. Generalisations</h3><p>A generalisation is a broad statement that describes the <b>main pattern</b> or trend, supported by evidence.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Good generalisation</b>\"Most international tourists visit Europe (about 50% of arrivals), while the Pacific receives fewer than 1%.\" – trend + region + figure.</div><div class=\"warn\">⚠ Do not just copy numbers – state the overall pattern first, then give evidence.</div><h3>3. Maps and graphs: BOLTSSNA</h3><table><tr><th>Letter</th><th>Maps (BOLTSSNA)</th><th>Graphs (BALTSSNA)</th></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Border</td><td>Border</td></tr><tr><td>O / A</td><td>Orientation (north arrow)</td><td>Axes labelled</td></tr><tr><td>L</td><td>Legend (key)</td><td>Legend</td></tr><tr><td>T</td><td>Title</td><td>Title</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>Scale</td><td>Scale</td></tr><tr><td>S</td><td>Source</td><td>Source</td></tr><tr><td>N</td><td>Name</td><td>Name</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Accuracy</td><td>Accuracy</td></tr></table><p>Choose the right graph: line graph for change over time, bar graph for comparing places, pie chart for parts of a whole, choropleth map for density.</p><h3>4. Cartoons and photographs</h3><p>Describe what you <b>see</b> (people, labels, symbols), then explain the <b>message</b> (what issue, whose point of view), then link to geographic ideas (cause, effect, perception).</p><h3>5. Case studies</h3><p>For each section, learn ONE named example with: <b>where</b> (country, region, latitude/longitude), <b>when</b>, <b>what happened</b> (with figures), <b>effects</b> on people and environment, <b>strategies</b> and how effective they were.</p><div class=\"tip\">💡 Pacific case studies score well: Cyclone Pam (Vanuatu 2015), Cyclone Winston (Fiji 2016), the 2009 Samoa tsunami, water shortage in South Tarawa, urbanisation of Betio, tourism in Fiji.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A good generalisation includes…", "o": ["one example only", "the overall pattern, where it occurs and a reason", "a definition", "a diagram"], "a": 1, "e": "Pattern + location + reason."}, {"q": "How many strategies are needed for 7 marks?", "o": ["One", "Two", "Four", "Six"], "a": 2, "e": "Four, well discussed, with case studies."}, {"q": "When reading a cartoon you should…", "o": ["describe the colours", "give evidence from it and explain the impact", "copy the caption", "skip it"], "a": 1, "e": "Evidence + explanation."}, {"q": "Suggested time per question", "o": ["10 min", "20 min", "36 min", "60 min"], "a": 2, "e": "5 questions in 3 hours."}]}, {"t": "Living with natural hazards (earthquakes, cyclones, tsunamis)", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define hazard terms (natural hazard, disaster, tropical cyclone, earthquake, epicentre, focus, tsunami, volcano, plate boundaries, hot spot, Richter scale)</li><li>describe the global distribution of earthquakes, volcanoes, cyclones and tsunamis</li><li>explain how plate movements and atmospheric processes cause them</li><li>describe effects on people and the environment</li><li>evaluate strategies to reduce negative effects using a case study for each hazard</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key terms</h3><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Natural hazard</td><td>a natural event that may harm people and property</td></tr><tr><td>Disaster</td><td>a hazard that actually causes major loss of life or property</td></tr><tr><td>Tropical cyclone</td><td>an intense low-pressure storm with winds over 63 km/h forming over warm tropical oceans (hurricane/typhoon elsewhere)</td></tr><tr><td>Earthquake</td><td>sudden shaking of the ground when stress is released along a fault or plate boundary</td></tr><tr><td>Focus / epicentre</td><td>point underground where it starts / point on the surface directly above</td></tr><tr><td>Tsunami</td><td>very long sea waves caused by an undersea earthquake, landslide or eruption</td></tr><tr><td>Hot spot</td><td>fixed plume of hot magma under a moving plate</td></tr></table><h3>2. Plate tectonics and distribution</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"80\" width=\"340\" height=\"70\" fill=\"#dbeafe\"/><polygon points=\"0,80 150,80 210,140 0,140\" fill=\"#c8b88a\"/><polygon points=\"170,80 340,80 340,140 230,140\" fill=\"#a89060\"/><path d=\"M165 80 L210 140\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"230,80 250,40 270,80\" fill=\"#8a5a3a\"/><path d=\"M250 40 L255 15\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"75\">ocean plate →</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"75\">← continental plate</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#c0392b\">trench</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"120\">melting → magma rises</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Convergent (destructive) boundary: the denser ocean plate is subducted, causing earthquakes and volcanoes</div></div><table><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Movement</th><th>Result</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Convergent (destructive)</td><td>plates collide, one subducts</td><td>trenches, volcanoes, strong earthquakes</td><td>Tonga Trench, Vanuatu, Japan</td></tr><tr><td>Divergent (constructive)</td><td>plates move apart</td><td>new crust, rift valleys, mid-ocean ridges</td><td>Mid-Atlantic Ridge, Iceland</td></tr><tr><td>Transform (conservative)</td><td>plates slide past</td><td>earthquakes, no volcanoes</td><td>San Andreas Fault, USA</td></tr></table><p>Most earthquakes and volcanoes lie in narrow belts along plate boundaries, especially the <b>Pacific \"Ring of Fire\"</b>. Hot-spot volcanoes form chains in the middle of plates (Hawai'i).</p><h3>3. How tropical cyclones form</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 140\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"115\" width=\"320\" height=\"25\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#fff\">warm sea over 26–27 °C</text><path d=\"M160 110 L160 40\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"160,35 154,48 166,48\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"168\" y=\"70\">warm moist air rises</text><ellipse cx=\"160\" cy=\"30\" rx=\"110\" ry=\"18\" fill=\"#ddd\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"34\">cloud, condensation releases heat</text><path d=\"M60 100 Q100 80 150 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M260 100 Q220 80 170 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"95\">air spirals in</text><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"100\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"90\">eye</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Cyclones need warm water, moist rising air, and the Coriolis effect (they form 5–20° from the equator, not on it)</div></div><p>In the southern hemisphere cyclones spin <b>clockwise</b> and usually move south/south-east. Season: November–April. They weaken over land or cold water. Kiribati (near the equator) rarely gets cyclones but suffers storm surges and king tides.</p><h3>4. Effects on people and the environment</h3><table><tr><th>Hazard</th><th>Effects on people</th><th>Effects on the environment</th></tr><tr><td>Cyclone</td><td>deaths, houses destroyed, homelessness, crops and food lost, water contaminated, disease, schools closed</td><td>trees flattened, coral broken, coastal erosion, saltwater in freshwater lens</td></tr><tr><td>Earthquake</td><td>deaths from collapsing buildings, injuries, power and water cut, displacement</td><td>landslides, liquefaction, tsunamis, land uplift</td></tr><tr><td>Volcano</td><td>deaths, buildings buried, ash damages crops and lungs; BUT fertile soils, tourism, geothermal power</td><td>lava flows, ash cloud, acid rain, new land</td></tr><tr><td>Tsunami</td><td>drowning, coastal villages destroyed</td><td>salt contamination, beach erosion, reef damage</td></tr></table><h3>5. Reducing the effects – and evaluating them</h3><table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How it helps</th><th>Limitations</th></tr><tr><td>Prediction and monitoring (satellites, seismographs)</td><td>gives warning time</td><td>earthquakes cannot be predicted exactly</td></tr><tr><td>Early warning systems (radio, SMS, sirens)</td><td>people evacuate in time</td><td>outer islands with poor communication</td></tr><tr><td>Hazard/risk maps and land-use zoning</td><td>keep people out of danger areas</td><td>people may still live there (land shortage)</td></tr><tr><td>Building design (cyclone straps, concrete shelters, earthquake-proof frames)</td><td>fewer collapses</td><td>expensive for poor families</td></tr><tr><td>Evacuation drills and emergency plans</td><td>people know what to do</td><td>only works if practised</td></tr><tr><td>Aid and rebuilding</td><td>faster recovery</td><td>slow to reach remote places; dependency</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Case study</b>Cyclone Pam, Vanuatu, 13–14 March 2015, Category 5, winds ~250 km/h. About 90% of Port Vila's buildings damaged, 75 000 people homeless, crops destroyed. Strategies: radio warnings and evacuation centres (only 11 deaths – effective), emergency aid from Australia/NZ (food, water, tarpaulins), rebuilding with stronger designs. Weakness: outer islands waited days for help.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "The point on the surface directly above the focus is the…", "o": ["focus", "epicentre", "fault", "crust"], "a": 1, "e": "The strongest shaking."}, {"q": "Most earthquakes occur…", "o": ["in the middle of plates", "along plate boundaries", "only in deserts", "randomly"], "a": 1, "e": "Ring of Fire."}, {"q": "Why is northern India at high risk?", "o": ["It has volcanoes", "The Indian plate collides with the Eurasian plate", "It is near the equator", "It has many rivers"], "a": 1, "e": "Collision → Himalayas."}, {"q": "Base isolators in buildings…", "o": ["predict earthquakes", "absorb shaking", "stop tsunamis", "are early warnings"], "a": 1, "e": "Earthquake-resistant design."}, {"q": "\"Drop, cover, hold\" is a…", "o": ["building code", "preparedness/education strategy", "warning system", "aid programme"], "a": 1, "e": "Education and drills."}], "w": 20}, {"t": "Use and management of water resources", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define water terms (renewable/non-renewable, hydrological cycle, infiltration, run-off, water table, surplus, scarcity, irrigation…)</li><li>describe global patterns of water surplus and scarcity</li><li>explain natural factors: latitude and rainfall type, temperature, seasons, relief, rock, vegetation</li><li>explain different uses of water and their positive and negative effects</li><li>evaluate water management strategies with a case study from a water-scarce area</li></ul></div><h3>1. The hydrological (water) cycle</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 160\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"120\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"145\" fill=\"#fff\">sea</text><polygon points=\"160,160 160,110 220,60 280,90 340,80 340,160\" fill=\"#8fbf6f\"/><ellipse cx=\"230\" cy=\"30\" rx=\"60\" ry=\"16\" fill=\"#ddd\"/><path d=\"M60 115 L60 50\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><polygon points=\"60,45 55,55 65,55\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"65\" y=\"80\">evaporation</text><path d=\"M100 30 L170 30\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"95\" y=\"22\">condensation, wind</text><g stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"><line x1=\"215\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"68\"/><line x1=\"223\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"218\" y2=\"68\"/><line x1=\"231\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"68\"/><line x1=\"239\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"234\" y2=\"68\"/><line x1=\"247\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"242\" y2=\"68\"/></g><text x=\"260\" y=\"58\">precipitation</text><path d=\"M270 95 Q220 110 165 125\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"118\">run-off</text><path d=\"M300 100 L300 140\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"150\">infiltration → groundwater</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"78\">transpiration</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The water cycle recycles fresh water: salt stays in the sea when water evaporates</div></div><p>Only about <b>2.5%</b> of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is ice. Groundwater and lakes provide most usable fresh water.</p><h3>2. Global distribution and natural factors</h3><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Water surplus</th><th>Water scarcity</th></tr><tr><td>Latitude / rainfall type</td><td>equator: convectional rain all year (Amazon, Congo)</td><td>20–30° N/S: sinking dry air (Sahara, Australia)</td></tr><tr><td>Temperature</td><td>moderate → less evaporation</td><td>hot → high evaporation</td></tr><tr><td>Seasons</td><td>rain all year</td><td>long dry season (monsoon areas)</td></tr><tr><td>Relief</td><td>windward mountain slopes (relief rain)</td><td>rain shadow on leeward side</td></tr><tr><td>Rock type</td><td>impermeable rock keeps surface water</td><td>porous rock/sand: water sinks away</td></tr><tr><td>Vegetation</td><td>forests return moisture and slow run-off</td><td>bare ground: fast run-off, high evaporation</td></tr></table><p>Atolls like Kiribati depend on rainwater and a thin <b>freshwater lens</b> floating on seawater under the islands.</p><h3>3. Uses of water and their effects</h3><table><tr><th>Use</th><th>Positive</th><th>Negative</th></tr><tr><td>Agriculture (irrigation)</td><td>more food, crops in dry season</td><td>waterlogging, salinity, aquifers run dry</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic/municipal</td><td>drinking, cooking, sanitation, health</td><td>pollution, water-borne disease (cholera, typhoid)</td></tr><tr><td>Industry</td><td>raw material, cooling, cleaning</td><td>pollution of rivers</td></tr><tr><td>Hydro-power</td><td>clean renewable electricity</td><td>dams flood land, displace people, trap silt</td></tr><tr><td>Recreation / transport</td><td>tourism, cheap shipping</td><td>pollution, oil spills</td></tr></table><p><b>Perception</b> differs: in Nauru or South Tarawa water is precious; in wet Viti Levu or the USA people may waste it.</p><h3>4. Managing water – and evaluating</h3><table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Example</th><th>Evaluation</th></tr><tr><td>Rainwater tanks</td><td>Kiribati, Tuvalu</td><td>cheap, but runs dry in droughts</td></tr><tr><td>Desalination</td><td>Saudi Arabia, Nauru, Betio plant</td><td>reliable but expensive (fuel, maintenance)</td></tr><tr><td>Protecting groundwater (water reserves, no building)</td><td>Bonriki and Buota reserves, Tarawa</td><td>effective but land disputes and overcrowding</td></tr><tr><td>Drip irrigation</td><td>Israel, Australia</td><td>saves water, high setup cost</td></tr><tr><td>Metering, rationing, fixing leaks</td><td>PUB South Tarawa</td><td>reduces waste, unpopular</td></tr><tr><td>Dams, multi-purpose river projects</td><td>Three Gorges (China), Monasavu (Fiji)</td><td>power + flood control but displacement and silt</td></tr><tr><td>Recycling waste water, composting toilets</td><td>Japan, Australia, Kiribati trials</td><td>saves fresh water, cultural resistance</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Case study (water scarcity)</b>South Tarawa: about 60 000 people on a narrow atoll; rainfall is variable (droughts in La Niña years); the freshwater lens is polluted by pit latrines and overpumped. Strategies: water reserves at Bonriki/Buota, rainwater tanks, desalination plant (2020s), water rationing by PUB, sanitation projects. Effectiveness: supply improved but still limited to a few hours a day and population growth keeps raising demand.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A renewable resource is one that…", "o": ["runs out quickly", "is naturally replaced", "is man-made", "is always salty"], "a": 1, "e": "E.g. fresh water through the water cycle."}, {"q": "The energy source of the water cycle is…", "o": ["the Moon", "the Sun", "wind", "the Earth's core"], "a": 1, "e": "Solar energy drives evaporation."}, {"q": "Water soaking into the ground is…", "o": ["run-off", "infiltration", "condensation", "transpiration"], "a": 1, "e": "It recharges groundwater."}, {"q": "Bonriki water reserve on Tarawa protects…", "o": ["the reef", "the freshwater lens", "fish stocks", "the airport"], "a": 1, "e": "Groundwater supply."}, {"q": "A strategy for a water-SURPLUS area", "o": ["desalination", "flood control levees and dams", "drip irrigation", "rainwater tanks"], "a": 1, "e": "They manage excess water."}], "w": 20}, {"t": "Consequences of population growth", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define population terms (birth/death rate, natural increase, density, dependency ratio, pyramid, migration, push–pull…)</li><li>explain population change = natural increase + net migration</li><li>construct and interpret the demographic transition model</li><li>explain physical, economic, social and political factors in distribution and growth</li><li>describe consequences and evaluate population policies</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key terms and calculations</h3><div class=\"key\">Natural increase = births − deaths<br>Rate of natural increase (%) = (birth rate − death rate) ÷ 10<br>Population growth = natural increase + net migration<br>Density = population ÷ area (people/km²)<br>Dependency ratio = (0–14 + 65+) ÷ (15–64) × 100</div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Birth rate 30‰, death rate 6‰ → natural increase 24‰ = 2.4% per year → doubling time ≈ 70 ÷ 2.4 ≈ 29 years.</div><h3>2. Demographic transition model</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 160\" font-size=\"10\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M30 30 L110 30 Q170 35 200 90 L330 95\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M30 35 Q60 40 80 90 Q100 105 330 105\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M30 120 Q90 120 150 60 Q210 20 330 18\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#bbb\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><text x=\"55\" y=\"145\">Stage 1</text><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#bbb\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"145\">Stage 2</text><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#bbb\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"145\">Stage 3</text><line x1=\"255\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#bbb\" stroke-dasharray=\"3,3\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"145\">Stage 4</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"25\" fill=\"#c0392b\">birth rate</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"85\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\">death rate</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">total population</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Stage 1 high BR and DR; Stage 2 DR falls (explosion); Stage 3 BR falls; Stage 4 both low</div></div><p>Most Pacific countries (Kiribati, Solomons) are in late Stage 2/early Stage 3: fast growth and a youthful population. Japan and much of Europe are in Stage 4/5: ageing or falling populations.</p><h3>3. Population pyramids</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 130\" font-size=\"10\"><g transform=\"translate(80,0)\"><rect x=\"-60\" y=\"110\" width=\"60\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"110\" width=\"60\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-52\" y=\"98\" width=\"52\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"98\" width=\"52\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-44\" y=\"86\" width=\"44\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"86\" width=\"44\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-36\" y=\"74\" width=\"36\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"74\" width=\"36\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-28\" y=\"62\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"62\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-20\" y=\"50\" width=\"20\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"50\" width=\"20\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-13\" y=\"38\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"38\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-7\" y=\"26\" width=\"7\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"26\" width=\"7\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-3\" y=\"14\" width=\"3\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"14\" width=\"3\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><text x=\"-40\" y=\"128\">youthful (Kiribati)</text></g><g transform=\"translate(245,0)\"><rect x=\"-25\" y=\"110\" width=\"25\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"110\" width=\"25\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-27\" y=\"98\" width=\"27\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"98\" width=\"27\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-30\" y=\"86\" width=\"30\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"86\" width=\"30\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-32\" y=\"74\" width=\"32\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"74\" width=\"32\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-33\" y=\"62\" width=\"33\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"62\" width=\"33\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-34\" y=\"50\" width=\"34\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"50\" width=\"34\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-32\" y=\"38\" width=\"32\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"38\" width=\"32\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-28\" y=\"26\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"26\" width=\"28\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><rect x=\"-20\" y=\"14\" width=\"20\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><rect x=\"0\" y=\"14\" width=\"20\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#e57373\"/><text x=\"-40\" y=\"128\">ageing (Japan)</text></g></svg><div class=\"cap\">Wide base = high birth rate; straight sides = low birth and death rates</div></div><h3>4. Factors affecting distribution and growth</h3><table><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Distribution (where people live)</th><th>Growth</th></tr><tr><td>Physical</td><td>flat fertile plains, water, mild climate attract; deserts, mountains, cold repel</td><td>disasters, disease</td></tr><tr><td>Economic</td><td>jobs in cities, industry, fertile farmland</td><td>cost of living, children as farm labour</td></tr><tr><td>Social/cultural</td><td>land ownership, family ties, tradition</td><td>religion, education of women, marriage age, health care, HIV/AIDS</td></tr><tr><td>Political</td><td>conflict, resettlement schemes (e.g. Kiribati to Line Islands)</td><td>population policies, war</td></tr></table><h3>5. Consequences and responses</h3><p><b>Effects of rapid growth</b>: deforestation, overcrowding (e.g. Betio – one of the most densely populated places in the Pacific), pollution, pressure on water and land, unemployment, demand for schools and clinics, conflicts over land. <b>Ageing populations</b>: fewer workers, high pension and health costs.</p><table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Evaluation</th></tr><tr><td>Family planning and contraception</td><td>effective where accepted; cultural/religious resistance</td></tr><tr><td>Education for girls and women</td><td>most effective long-term; takes a generation</td></tr><tr><td>Government policies (China one-child, 1979–2015)</td><td>cut births by ~400 m but caused ageing and gender imbalance</td></tr><tr><td>Migration / resettlement (Kiribati to Kiritimati, labour schemes in NZ & Australia)</td><td>reduces crowding, remittances; brain drain</td></tr><tr><td>Incentives to have more children (Japan, Singapore)</td><td>used in ageing countries; limited success</td></tr></table>", "quiz": [{"q": "In Stage 2 of the DTM…", "o": ["both rates are low", "the death rate falls quickly while the birth rate stays high", "the birth rate falls", "the population declines"], "a": 1, "e": "Rapid growth."}, {"q": "A wide base on a population pyramid shows…", "o": ["an ageing population", "a high birth rate", "a low birth rate", "many elderly"], "a": 1, "e": "Typical of developing countries."}, {"q": "China's one-child policy is an example of a…", "o": ["cultural factor", "political factor", "physical factor", "economic factor"], "a": 1, "e": "Government policy."}, {"q": "A strategy for a country with NEGATIVE growth", "o": ["a one-child policy", "baby bonuses and paid parental leave", "free contraception campaigns", "emigration"], "a": 1, "e": "Encourages births."}, {"q": "\"Economically active\" means people who…", "o": ["are retired", "work or are looking for work", "are under 15", "own businesses only"], "a": 1, "e": "The labour force."}], "w": 20}, {"t": "Urbanisation and urban environments", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define urban terms (urbanisation, urban sprawl, CBD, shanty town/squatter settlement, ghetto, push–pull, counter-urbanisation…)</li><li>describe world patterns of urbanisation and urban growth</li><li>explain causes in developed and developing countries</li><li>describe urban problems and their effects</li><li>evaluate urban planning strategies</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key ideas</h3><p><b>Urbanisation</b> = increase in the <b>proportion</b> of people living in towns. Urban growth = increase in the <b>number</b>. Today over 55% of the world lives in urban areas.</p><table><tr><th>Developed countries</th><th>Developing countries</th></tr><tr><td>already 75–90% urban, slow growth</td><td>30–60% urban, growing fast</td></tr><tr><td>caused by the Industrial Revolution (factory jobs)</td><td>caused by rural–urban migration and high natural increase</td></tr><tr><td>now some counter-urbanisation (moving to the countryside)</td><td>megacities: Lagos, Dhaka, Mumbai</td></tr></table><h3>2. Push and pull factors</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 110\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"20\" width=\"120\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e6f4e2\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"40\">RURAL / OUTER ISLAND</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"58\">push: few jobs, poor</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"72\">schools and clinics,</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"86\">land shortage</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"58\" font-size=\"20\">→</text><rect x=\"175\" y=\"20\" width=\"145\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fde2e2\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"185\" y=\"40\">TOWN (South Tarawa)</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"58\">pull: jobs, wages,</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"72\">USP/KIT, hospital,</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"86\">shops, bright lights</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Rural–urban drift</div></div><h3>3. Urban problems and effects</h3><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Cause</th><th>Effects</th></tr><tr><td>Overcrowding, informal settlements</td><td>migration faster than housing</td><td>poor sanitation, disease (diarrhoea, TB), fire risk</td></tr><tr><td>Unemployment</td><td>more people than jobs</td><td>poverty, crime, social problems</td></tr><tr><td>Traffic congestion</td><td>too many vehicles, narrow roads (one main road on Tarawa)</td><td>lost time, accidents, pollution</td></tr><tr><td>Pollution and waste</td><td>rubbish, sewage, lack of services</td><td>polluted lagoon, health risks, reef damage</td></tr><tr><td>Water shortage</td><td>high demand on limited supply</td><td>rationing, contaminated wells</td></tr></table><h3>4. Planning strategies – and evaluating</h3><table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Example</th><th>Evaluation</th></tr><tr><td>Green belts</td><td>London</td><td>stops sprawl, raises house prices</td></tr><tr><td>New towns</td><td>Milton Keynes (UK)</td><td>reduce crowding; some became commuter towns</td></tr><tr><td>Public transport, congestion charge</td><td>London 2003</td><td>cut traffic 15–30% at first</td></tr><tr><td>Urban renewal</td><td>London Docklands</td><td>new jobs but gentrification pushes poor out</td></tr><tr><td>Site-and-service schemes, low-cost housing</td><td>Fiji Housing Authority, Kiribati</td><td>improves conditions; slow and underfunded</td></tr><tr><td>Develop outer islands (decentralisation)</td><td>Kiribati Kiritimati development</td><td>reduces push factors; costly, slow</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Case study</b>South Tarawa (Betio): ~50% of Kiribati's population on 16 km², densities over 10 000/km² in Betio. Causes: outer-island push factors, jobs and services in Tarawa. Problems: overcrowding, water shortage, sewage in the lagoon, unemployment. Strategies: sanitation and water projects (ADB/World Bank), road upgrade, outer-island development – partly effective but population keeps growing.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A road built to divert traffic around a congested town centre is a…", "o": ["ring fence", "bypass", "cul-de-sac", "flyover"], "a": 1, "e": "It reduces CBD congestion."}, {"q": "Lack of jobs on the outer islands is a…", "o": ["pull factor", "push factor", "process", "strategy"], "a": 1, "e": "It pushes people away."}, {"q": "Moving from the outer islands to South Tarawa is…", "o": ["counter-urbanisation", "rural–urban drift", "emigration", "suburbanisation"], "a": 1, "e": "Rural → urban."}, {"q": "Acid rain is caused mainly by…", "o": ["CO₂ from breathing", "sulfur dioxide from factories and vehicles", "sea spray", "rainwater tanks"], "a": 1, "e": "SO₂ and NOx."}, {"q": "Which countries are urbanising faster now?", "o": ["developed", "developing", "neither", "both equally"], "a": 1, "e": "Rapid growth from a lower level."}], "w": 20}, {"t": "Geographic perspectives on tourism", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define tourism terms (tourist, mass tourism, eco-tourism, amenities, carrying capacity, leakage, multiplier effect…)</li><li>describe world and Pacific patterns of tourism</li><li>explain physical and human factors that attract tourists</li><li>describe positive and negative effects on people and environment</li><li>evaluate strategies for managing tourism</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key terms</h3><table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Tourist</td><td>someone travelling away from home for at least 24 hours for leisure</td></tr><tr><td>Mass tourism</td><td>very large numbers to the same places (package holidays)</td></tr><tr><td>Eco-tourism</td><td>small-scale tourism that protects nature and benefits locals</td></tr><tr><td>Tourist amenities</td><td>facilities for tourists: hotels, restaurants, tours</td></tr><tr><td>Leakage</td><td>tourist money that leaves the country (foreign hotel owners, imports)</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplier effect</td><td>tourist spending creates more jobs and income in other businesses</td></tr><tr><td>Carrying capacity</td><td>the most visitors a place can take without damage</td></tr></table><h3>2. Patterns and factors</h3><p>Europe receives about half of all international arrivals; Asia–Pacific is growing fastest. Fiji is the Pacific leader (~900 000 visitors a year); Kiribati gets only a few thousand (fishing, diving, WWII history at Tarawa).</p><table><tr><th>Physical attractions</th><th>Human attractions</th></tr><tr><td>beaches, coral reefs, warm climate</td><td>culture, festivals, history (WWII sites)</td></tr><tr><td>volcanoes, waterfalls, wildlife</td><td>hotels, airports, flights, safety</td></tr><tr><td>clean environment</td><td>marketing, visa rules, cost</td></tr></table><p>Growth factors: higher incomes, cheaper flights, paid holidays, the internet. Declines: disasters, disease (COVID-19), conflict, economic recession.</p><h3>3. Effects of tourism</h3><table><tr><th></th><th>Positive</th><th>Negative</th></tr><tr><td>Economic</td><td>jobs, foreign exchange, government tax, multiplier effect</td><td>leakage, seasonal jobs, low wages, prices rise</td></tr><tr><td>Social</td><td>handicraft markets, culture revived, better roads and services</td><td>loss of culture, crime, drugs, overcrowding</td></tr><tr><td>Environmental</td><td>money for conservation (marine parks)</td><td>reef damage, rubbish, sewage, mangroves cleared, water shortages</td></tr></table><h3>4. Managing tourism – and evaluating</h3><table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Example</th><th>Evaluation</th></tr><tr><td>Eco-tourism lodges</td><td>Fiji, Samoa beach fales</td><td>low impact, money stays local; small income</td></tr><tr><td>Marine protected areas, dive fees</td><td>PIPA (Kiribati), Fiji shark reserves</td><td>protect reefs; hard to police</td></tr><tr><td>Village/homestay tourism</td><td>Vanuatu, Samoa</td><td>spreads benefits; quality and marketing weak</td></tr><tr><td>Laws: environmental impact assessments, building limits</td><td>Fiji, Cook Islands</td><td>reduce damage if enforced</td></tr><tr><td>Visitor limits / carrying capacity</td><td>Galápagos</td><td>protects nature; fewer jobs</td></tr></table><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Case study</b>Fiji: tourism = about 40% of GDP; resorts on the Coral Coast and Mamanuca islands. Benefits: 100 000+ jobs, foreign exchange. Problems: leakage to foreign owners, reef damage, cultural change. Strategies: Fiji Tourism Development Plan, eco-tourism, marine reserves (e.g. Navini, Shark Reef Marine Reserve). COVID-19 (2020–21) showed the danger of depending on one industry.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Accommodation in tourism means…", "o": ["transport", "places to stay overnight", "souvenirs", "tour guides"], "a": 1, "e": "Hotels, resorts, guesthouses."}, {"q": "Most visitors to the Cook Islands come from New Zealand because of…", "o": ["distance and historical ties", "cheap food", "its volcanoes", "snow"], "a": 0, "e": "Free association with NZ."}, {"q": "Profits from foreign-owned hotels leaving the country is called…", "o": ["multiplier", "leakage", "ecotourism", "carrying capacity"], "a": 1, "e": "It reduces local benefit."}, {"q": "A natural (physical) factor that reduced world tourism", "o": ["higher incomes", "a tsunami or pandemic", "cheaper flights", "visa-free travel"], "a": 1, "e": "Disasters and COVID-19."}, {"q": "Small-scale tourism run by local villages is…", "o": ["mass tourism", "community-based ecotourism", "cruise tourism", "business travel"], "a": 1, "e": "Benefits stay local."}], "w": 20}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC Geography 2025.pdf', note:'Most questions use maps, graphs and cartoons. Open the original paper to see the figures. The answers below are model answers.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Living with natural hazards (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "an earthquake zone".',a:'An area of the Earth\'s crust where earthquakes happen frequently, usually along the boundaries of tectonic plates.'},
{n:'2.',q:'One generalisation about the distribution of earthquake-prone areas.',a:'Most earthquake-prone areas lie in narrow belts along plate boundaries, especially around the edge of the Pacific Ocean (the Ring of Fire), where oceanic plates are subducted beneath other plates (e.g. the Pacific plate under the American plates and Japan), producing strong earthquakes.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Why are very high damage risk zones concentrated in northern India?',a:'Northern India lies along the collision boundary where the Indo-Australian plate pushes north into the Eurasian plate (forming the Himalayas). Enormous stress builds up and is released in powerful earthquakes, and dense populations increase the damage risk.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Name parts A and B.',a:'A = <b>Focus</b> (hypocentre), where the rocks break underground.\nB = <b>Epicentre</b>, on the surface directly above the focus.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Explain how an earthquake is caused.',a:'Tectonic plates move slowly, but friction makes them lock together at the plate boundary or fault. Stress builds up in the rocks until they suddenly break and slip at the focus. The stored energy is released as seismic waves, which travel outward and shake the ground, strongest at the epicentre.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Two negative effects of the earthquake on the people of Vanuatu.',a:'1. Loss of life and injuries: at least 10 killed and over 200 injured.\n2. Destruction of homes, schools and hospitals, leaving families homeless, without access to safe drinking water, food and medical care.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Two causes that worsened these effects.',a:'1. Aftershocks and landslides caused more damage and made rescue dangerous.\n2. Communication lines and the internet were cut, so rescue and aid were slow and hard to coordinate. (Also: Vanuatu faces repeated disasters, limited resources and buildings that are not earthquake-resistant.)'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four strategies to reduce the negative effects of earthquakes, with case studies. (7)',a:'1. <b>Earthquake-resistant buildings and codes</b>: rubber base isolators, steel frames and cross-bracing let buildings sway without collapsing (Japan, NZ after the 2011 Christchurch quake).\n2. <b>Early warning systems</b>: seismometers send alerts seconds before shaking, stopping trains and lifts; tsunami warnings by text and siren (Japan; the Pacific Tsunami Warning Centre warns Vanuatu, Fiji and Kiribati).\n3. <b>Education and drills</b>: regular "Drop, Cover, Hold" drills and the national ShakeOut in NZ and Japan; Red Cross community preparedness training in Vanuatu.\n4. <b>Hazard mapping and land-use zoning</b>: maps like India\'s risk zones guide where to build, keeping hospitals and schools off faults and landslide slopes (California, NZ).\n(Also accepted: emergency kits, evacuation centres, NDMO planning, international aid.)'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Water resources (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "renewable resource".',a:'A natural resource that is replaced naturally, so it will not run out if it is used wisely (e.g. fresh water renewed by the water cycle).'},
{n:'2.',q:'One generalisation about physical water scarcity areas.',a:'Physical water scarcity is concentrated in the hot, dry subtropical deserts (North Africa, the Middle East, central Asia, Australia, the south-west USA), where rainfall is low and evaporation is high, so supply cannot meet demand.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Construct the water cycle diagram.',a:'Draw the sea, land, a tree and a cloud. Label: the Sun (energy source) → evaporation (arrow up from the sea) → transpiration (arrow up from the tree) → condensation (cloud) → precipitation (rain falling) → run-off (arrow over the land surface back to the sea) → infiltration (arrow into the ground to groundwater).'},
{n:'4.',q:'One continent with a water surplus and one physical factor.',a:'<b>South America</b>: it lies on the equator, where intense heating causes convectional rainfall almost every day, and the Amazon rainforest and river system store and return huge amounts of water. (South-East Asia is also accepted: monsoon rains.)'},
{n:'5.',q:'One country with physical water scarcity and two physical causes.',a:'<b>Saudi Arabia</b>: 1. very low rainfall (desert, subtropical high pressure); 2. extreme heat causes high evaporation, and there are no permanent rivers. (Also: Egypt, Libya, Australia.)'},
{n:'6.',q:'Two water uses shown in the cartoon.',a:'e.g. sea transport/shipping and fishing (or recreation, industry, depending on the cartoon).'},
{n:'7.',q:'One positive and one negative water use.',a:'Positive: sea transport carries trade goods cheaply between countries and islands, supporting development (e.g. cargo ships to Betio port).\nNegative: ships and industry pollute the water with oil spills, waste and plastics, harming marine life and the people who depend on fish.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Two examples of water management in scarcity areas and two in surplus areas. (7)',a:'<b>Scarcity</b>:\n1. Rainwater harvesting: roof gutters and tanks at homes and schools across Kiribati and Tuvalu store rain for dry periods.\n2. Desalination: removing salt from seawater (Saudi Arabia, Israel; the desalination plant in South Tarawa), giving a reliable supply during droughts. (Also: protecting the water reserves at Bonriki and Buota, drip irrigation.)\n<b>Surplus</b>:\n3. Dams and reservoirs store floodwater for hydro-electricity and dry-season use (Three Gorges Dam, China).\n4. Flood defences: levees, dykes and storm-surge barriers (the Netherlands\' Delta Works) protect low-lying land from too much water.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Population growth (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "economically active" and give an example.',a:'People of working age (about 15–64) who are working or actively looking for work, e.g. a nurse, a fisherman or a shop assistant.'},
{n:'2.',q:'One generalisation about population growth in developed vs developing countries, 2005–2025.',a:'Developing countries\' populations grew rapidly because fertility rates stayed high, while developed countries grew very slowly (or not at all) because each woman has fewer children and the population is ageing.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Construct a simple DTM.',a:'Vertical axis: birth and death rates (per 1000). Horizontal axis: time (stages of development). Draw a birth rate line (high, then falling in Stage 3, low in Stage 4) and a death rate line (high in Stage 1, falling sharply in Stage 2, low after). Label Stages 1–4. Shade the gap where the birth rate is above the death rate as natural increase; where the birth rate drops below the death rate (Stage 5), label natural decrease.'},
{n:'4.',q:'An economic factor shown by the developed-country pyramid.',a:'The high cost of raising and educating children, and women working in well-paid careers, mean couples have fewer children → a narrow base (low birth rate). Good pensions and health care mean people live longer → a wide top (ageing).'},
{n:'5.',q:'A cultural factor shown by the developing-country pyramid.',a:'Large families are valued: children are seen as a blessing, as help with work and as security for parents in old age, and some religions discourage contraception. This leads to high birth rates and a wide base.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Two pieces of evidence that the cartoon shows a political impact in China.',a:'1. It mentions the government\'s "one-child policy" (a law made by the state).\n2. It shows the Chinese flag and the leader, meaning the policy comes from the government.'},
{n:'7.',q:'How this political impact affects people in China.',a:'The birth rate fell sharply, so China now has a shrinking workforce and a rapidly ageing population. Each worker must support more elderly relatives (the 4-2-1 problem), pension and health costs rise, and there is a gender imbalance (more men than women) because boys were preferred, making it hard for many men to marry.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four strategies for a country with negative population growth. (7)',a:'1. <b>Financial incentives</b>: baby bonuses and family allowances (Singapore\'s Baby Bonus; Russia\'s "maternity capital" for second children).\n2. <b>Family-friendly policies</b>: long paid parental leave, affordable childcare and flexible work (Sweden, France) so parents can have children and keep their jobs.\n3. <b>Immigration</b>: attracting foreign workers to fill labour shortages (Germany, NZ, Japan\'s new skilled-worker visas).\n4. <b>Adapting to ageing</b>: raising the retirement age, encouraging older people to work, and using robots and automation (Japan). China also replaced the one-child policy with two- and three-child policies.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Urbanisation (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "bypass" with an example.',a:'A road built around a town or city centre so that through traffic can avoid the congested CBD (e.g. a ring road or bypass around a busy city such as Suva).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Level of urbanisation in 2025: Japan, USA, developing countries.',a:'Japan ≈ <b>94%</b>; USA ≈ <b>83%</b>; developing countries ≈ <b>47%</b> (read from the graph).'},
{n:'3.',q:'Compare urbanisation in developed and developing countries, 2025–2050.',a:'Similarity: both will continue to urbanise. Difference: Japan and the USA are already highly urbanised, so their levels will rise only slowly. Developing countries start lower but will rise much faster because of high birth rates and strong rural–urban drift.'},
{n:'4.',q:'One rural push factor and one urban pull factor.',a:'Push: lack of jobs, schools and medical facilities in rural areas (outer islands).\nPull: more job opportunities, better hospitals and education in the city.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Explain one process of urbanisation.',a:'<b>Rural–urban drift</b>: large numbers of people leave rural areas because of hardship (push factors) and move to towns in search of a better life (pull factors). In Kiribati, migration from the outer islands to South Tarawa has made Betio one of the most crowded places in the Pacific.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Type of urbanisation impact in the cartoon.',a:'<b>Air (and water) pollution</b>: exhaust fumes from heavy traffic and gases such as sulfur dioxide from factories create smog. Industries also dump chemical waste into rivers.'},
{n:'7.',q:'How this impact affects people and the environment.',a:'People: breathing polluted air causes asthma, lung disease and cancer, and polluted water spreads disease.\nEnvironment: sulfur dioxide forms acid rain, which damages crops, forests and buildings and kills life in lakes. Greenhouse gases add to global warming.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four examples of urban planning and management in developing countries. (7)',a:'1. <b>Zoning and land-use planning</b>: separate industrial, residential and commercial zones to reduce pollution and crowding (e.g. South Tarawa land-use plans).\n2. <b>Bypasses and public transport</b>: reduce traffic congestion and pollution (Curitiba, Brazil\'s bus rapid transit; road upgrades on South Tarawa).\n3. <b>Slum upgrading and site-and-service schemes</b>: give squatter areas water, sanitation and secure land (Suva, Fiji; Port Moresby).\n4. <b>Decentralisation and new towns</b>: develop the outer islands and growth centres so fewer people move to the capital (Kiribati\'s Kiritimati development).\n(Also: waste management and recycling, e.g. Kiribati\'s "Kaoki Maange".)'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Tourism (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "accommodation".',a:'The places where tourists stay overnight, providing a room or bed, bathroom facilities and often food (hotels, resorts, motels, guesthouses, homestays).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Main origins of international tourists to Kiribati.',a:'Mostly from other <b>Pacific Islands (about 31.4%)</b>, e.g. Fiji and Tuvalu, followed by Australia/New Zealand and Asia. Many visit for family, business, church or government work, and for sport fishing on Kiritimati.'},
{n:'3.',q:'One generalisation about origins and destinations for the Cook Islands and Fiji.',a:'Tourists mainly come from nearby countries with strong historical ties and direct flights: New Zealand supplies about 66% of the Cook Islands\' visitors (the two are in free association and Cook Islanders hold NZ citizenship), while Australia is Fiji\'s main source because it is close and has frequent flights.', flag:'The marking scheme calls the Cook Islands a "mandated territory under New Zealand". It is a self-governing state in free association with NZ.'},
{n:'4.',q:'One economic and one political factor in world tourism growth (1990–2019).',a:'Economic: rising incomes and cheaper air travel (budget airlines) in wealthy countries gave people more money for travel.\nPolitical: peace and political stability in destinations, plus easier visas and open borders, encouraged travel.'},
{n:'5.',q:'One physical factor that caused tourism declines.',a:'Natural disasters and disease, e.g. the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami destroyed resorts in Thailand and Sri Lanka, and the COVID-19 pandemic (2020) closed borders (just after the graph period). Cyclones also reduce Pacific tourism.'},
{n:'6.',q:'One economic benefit and one drawback of tourism (cartoon).',a:'Benefit: tourism creates jobs and income for local people (hotel work, guides, handicraft sales) and earns foreign exchange.\nDrawback: much of the money "leaks" out to foreign hotel owners and imported goods, and local prices rise, so locals gain less than expected.'},
{n:'7.',q:'One environmental benefit and one drawback of tourism (cartoon).',a:'Benefit: entrance fees and tourist interest pay for national parks and marine reserves, encouraging conservation.\nDrawback: large numbers of tourists bring litter, sewage, trampling and coral damage, polluting beaches and destroying habitats.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four tourism planning and management examples in Pacific Island countries. (7)',a:'1. <b>Ecotourism and community-based tourism</b>: village homestays and beach fale (Samoa, Fiji, Vanuatu) keep money in local communities and respect culture.\n2. <b>Marine protected areas</b>: the Phoenix Islands Protected Area (Kiribati) and Palau\'s marine sanctuary protect reefs and charge fees that fund conservation.\n3. <b>Regulations</b>: limits on visitor numbers, beach setback rules, waste and sewage standards for resorts (Fiji\'s environmental impact assessments; Palau\'s Eco-Pledge for visitors).\n4. <b>Reducing leakage</b>: train locals for management jobs, buy local food and crafts, and support local ownership, so more income stays in the country.'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC Geography Q&A.pdf', note:'Model answers in plain words, using the facts from the paper\'s figures (checked against the MOE answer schedule). For "case study" questions, learn ONE named example well. ⚠ marks errors in the schedule.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Living with natural hazards (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "hot spot"',a:'A <b>fixed, very hot area in the mantle</b> under a moving plate. Magma rises from it and punches through the plate, forming volcanoes. As the plate moves over the hot spot, it leaves a <b>chain of volcanic islands</b>, youngest over the hot spot and oldest furthest away (e.g. Hawai\'i).'},
{n:'2.',q:'Describe the location and distribution of the volcanic islands (black circles)',a:'<b>Location</b>: inside the Pacific Plate over hot spots (e.g. Hawai\'i in the north, the Marquesas in the centre, the Caroline Islands in the west, Samoa near the plate edge), and along the plate boundaries.\n<b>Distribution</b>: they form <b>long lines (linear chains)</b> running north-west, because the Pacific Plate moves north-west over fixed hot spots. Many are also <b>concentrated along the plate margins</b> (the "Ring of Fire"), where the crust is weak.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Direction of Cyclone Kevin; why did Cyclone Judy strengthen (157 → 194 km/h)?',a:'Kevin moved <b>south-east</b> from Vanuatu. (⚠ The MOE schedule also says "north-west", but on the map Kevin heads south-east, away from Vanuatu.)\nJudy got stronger because it passed over <b>warmer ocean water</b> (above 26–27 °C). The warm water gives more <b>evaporation and moist, rising air</b>. When this moisture condenses it releases heat energy, which fuels the cyclone and makes the winds faster. There was no land to slow it down.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Type of plate movement; feature Z',a:'<b>Convergent (destructive) plate boundary</b>: two plates move towards each other and the denser ocean plate is subducted.\nZ = an <b>ocean trench</b> (the deepest part of the sea floor, where the plate goes down).\n⚠ The MOE schedule calls this "constructive", which is wrong. Constructive means plates moving apart.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Explain how volcanoes are created',a:'1. Two plates collide, and the <b>heavier oceanic plate sinks (subducts)</b> under the lighter plate.\n2. As it goes down into the hot mantle, friction, heat and water from the plate make the rock <b>melt into magma</b>.\n3. The magma is <b>lighter</b> than the solid rock around it, so it <b>rises</b> through cracks and weak zones in the crust.\n4. Pressure builds in a magma chamber until the magma <b>erupts at the surface</b> as lava, ash and gas. Layers of these build up into a volcano.'},
{n:'6.',q:'One major impact of Cyclone Harold on people in Vanuatu, and its cause',a:'e.g. <b>Homelessness</b>: Harold\'s very strong winds (Category 5) <b>blew roofs off and destroyed over 17 000 houses</b>, leaving about <b>87 000 people homeless</b>. They had to crowd into evacuation centres, which caused stress and safety problems.\n(Other impacts: 885 schools damaged, so 40 000 children were out of school; crops destroyed, so food shortages; 37 health centres damaged and diarrhoea spread from contaminated water.)'},
{n:'7.',q:'One negative effect of tropical cyclones on the environment',a:'e.g. <b>Contamination of fresh water</b>: heavy rain and storm surges flood wells and the <b>freshwater lens with seawater and sewage</b>. The water becomes salty and polluted, causing diarrhoea, cholera and typhoid, and it can take months to recover.\n(Others: coral reefs broken by waves; coastal erosion; trees and crops flattened; mangroves destroyed.)'},
{n:'8.',q:'Cyclone case study: (a) location (b) three effects and three strategies',a:'Model case study: <b>Cyclone Pam, March 2015</b>.\n(a) <b>Vanuatu</b>, in the south-west Pacific (about 15–20° S, 168° E), about 1700 km west of Fiji. It hit Efate and the southern islands (Tanna, Erromango) and also affected Tuvalu and Kiribati.\n(b) Effects: 1. Winds of about <b>250 km/h</b> destroyed about 90% of buildings in Port Vila. 2. <b>Crops and food gardens destroyed</b>, causing food shortages. 3. <b>Storm surges and flooding</b> in Tuvalu and Kiribati contaminated water and damaged homes.\nStrategies: 1. <b>Early warning</b> by radio and SMS, so people moved to evacuation centres and few died. 2. <b>Stronger building codes</b> (cyclone straps, concrete shelters). 3. <b>Emergency aid and rebuilding</b> (food, water, tarpaulins from Australia, NZ and the Red Cross) and replanting fast-growing crops.\nEvaluate: warnings saved lives, but poor outer-island communication slowed help, and aid did not reach everyone quickly.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Water resources (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "cultural resource"',a:'A resource whose value comes from <b>human knowledge, technology and decisions</b>: people decide how to use it. For example, water becomes a resource when people build dams, wells or irrigation to use it.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Pattern of safe drinking-water access in Africa',a:'Access is <b>lowest in Sub-Saharan Africa</b> (central and east, e.g. Somalia and Ethiopia), where often less than 50–75% of people have basic drinking water. North Africa and southern Africa have <b>higher access</b>. It is an uneven pattern: poorer and drier countries have the least.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Name the process; how does it sustain fresh water?',a:'The <b>water (hydrological) cycle</b>.\nThe sun\'s heat <b>evaporates</b> water from the sea and plants transpire. Water vapour rises, <b>condenses</b> into clouds, and winds carry the clouds over land. Water falls as <b>precipitation</b> (fresh water: salt stays behind in the sea). It then runs off into rivers and lakes or <b>infiltrates</b> the ground to refill aquifers and the freshwater lens, and it returns to the sea. The cycle constantly <b>recycles and renews</b> fresh water.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Water situation at A, and processes causing it',a:'A = a <b>water surplus</b> area (e.g. the equatorial rainforest).\nProcesses: <b>high rainfall (convectional rain)</b>, <b>high evaporation and transpiration</b> from dense forest feeding more clouds, and rising warm moist air (low pressure). Precipitation is greater than evaporation.'},
{n:'5.',q:'Two causes of the situation at B (desert, water deficit)',a:'1. <b>Sinking dry air (high pressure)</b> around 20–30° latitude stops clouds forming, so there is very little rain.\n2. <b>Evaporation is far greater than precipitation</b> because of strong sun and heat. With few plants there is little transpiration and moisture, so it stays dry. (Cold ocean currents and rain shadows also add to this.)'},
{n:'6.',q:'Two pieces of evidence of sustainable water use in the photo',a:'e.g. water is applied <b>directly to the crop rows</b> (drip or channel irrigation, little waste); <b>water storage</b> (tanks or a reservoir) to save water for the dry season; regulated or shared use.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Main water use shown',a:'<b>Agriculture: irrigation of crops</b>. Water is taken from a river or reservoir and spread over the fields to grow food during the dry season.'},
{n:'8.',q:'(a) Three ways water is managed in water-surplus countries (b) evaluate them',a:'(a) 1. <b>Dams and reservoirs</b> (e.g. Three Gorges Dam, China) store floodwater, generate hydro-electricity and control floods.\n2. <b>Flood control</b>: levees, floodways and river straightening (e.g. the Mississippi, USA).\n3. <b>Water transfer and treatment</b>: pipelines carry surplus water to drier areas (e.g. the South–North Water Transfer in China), and treatment plants keep rivers clean.\n(b) Dams give power and reduce floods <b>but</b> displaced over 1 million people and trap sediment. Levees protect towns <b>but</b> can fail in big floods (New Orleans 2005). Transfer schemes supply cities <b>but</b> are very costly and can harm the river ecosystems they take from. Overall they are effective when well maintained, but have social and environmental costs.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Population growth (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "population explosion"',a:'A <b>very rapid increase in population</b>, which happens when the <b>death rate falls quickly</b> (better medicine, clean water) while the <b>birth rate stays high</b>.'},
{n:'2.',q:'World population in 2025, and the prediction for 2050–2075',a:'About <b>8.2 billion</b> in 2025 (from the graph).\nFrom 2050 the growth rate keeps <b>slowing down</b>, reaching almost <b>zero growth around 2075</b> (the population levels off at about 10 billion). The cause is <b>falling fertility rates</b>: more education for women, family planning and urbanisation mean families have fewer children.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Population density at G',a:'G (South/East Asia, e.g. India–Bangladesh) has <b>very high density, over 1000 people per km²</b>. It is a clustered pattern on fertile river plains and deltas, with flat land, plenty of water and a warm, wet monsoon climate for growing rice.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Most and least populated regions in 2020',a:'Most: <b>Asia</b>. Least: <b>Oceania</b>.'},
{n:'5.',q:'One physical factor that makes Asia so populated (with an example)',a:'<b>Fertile river plains</b>. For example, the <b>Yangtze River plain in China</b> (or the Ganges plain in India) has flat land, rich alluvial soil from floods and plenty of water. This supports intensive rice farming that feeds millions of people. The river is also used for transport and hydro-power, which attracts industry and more people.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Two negative impacts in the cartoon',a:'1. <b>Overuse (overexploitation) of natural resources</b> such as forests, water and fossil fuels.\n2. <b>Pollution and environmental degradation</b>: deforestation, greenhouse gas emissions and waste.'},
{n:'7.',q:'How population growth causes this (with an example)',a:'More people need more <b>food, energy and land</b>. Forests are cleared for farms and housing (e.g. in the <b>Amazon</b>, Brazil), and more coal, oil and gas are burned for electricity and transport. This releases more <b>greenhouse gases</b>, causing global warming. The result is climate change, loss of habitats and more waste and pollution.'},
{n:'8.',q:'(a) Three strategies for managing rapid population growth (b) evaluate them',a:'Model country: <b>China</b> (also India or Kiribati).\n(a) 1. <b>One-child policy (1979–2015)</b>: fines and lost benefits for having more children, and rewards for one child.\n2. <b>Family planning</b>: free contraception and education about smaller families (India, Kiribati).\n3. <b>Education and better status for women</b>: girls stay in school longer, marry later and have fewer children.\n(b) The one-child policy <b>worked</b> (it prevented about 400 million births) <b>but</b> caused an ageing population, more boys than girls and human-rights problems, so it was ended. Family planning is <b>slower but accepted</b>, though it depends on cultural and religious beliefs. Education is the most <b>effective long-term</b> strategy, but takes a generation to show results.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Urbanisation (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "ghettoes"',a:'Poor, run-down parts of a city where a <b>minority group</b> (by race, religion or culture) is <b>forced or pressured to live separately</b>. They usually have poor housing, high unemployment and crime.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Two generalisations about urbanisation, 1950–2050',a:'1. <b>Developed regions</b> are already highly urban and are rising <b>slowly</b> (about 56% in 1950 → about 88% by 2050).\n2. <b>Less developed regions</b> are rising <b>rapidly</b> (about 18% in 1950 → about 65% by 2050). Most urban growth now happens in Asia and Africa.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Causes of those patterns',a:'Developed countries urbanised early (the Industrial Revolution), so there are few rural people left to move and growth is slow. In developing countries, <b>rural–urban migration</b> (push: poverty, few jobs, poor services in villages; pull: jobs, schools, hospitals and better pay in towns) plus <b>high natural increase</b> in the cities makes urban populations grow quickly.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Causes of urbanisation in an industrialised city (London)',a:'<b>Industrialisation</b>: factories (and now offices and services) created many jobs, pulling people in from the countryside. Better transport, health care and wages kept the city growing. Today London grows mainly from <b>international migration</b> and service jobs (finance, tourism).'},
{n:'5.',q:'How Suva\'s causes differ',a:'Suva has <b>few factories</b>. People come mainly because of <b>push factors in rural areas and outer islands</b>: few jobs, poor schools and health care, natural disasters. They are also pulled by government jobs, education (USP) and services. Growth is faster than the number of jobs and houses, so <b>informal settlements</b> (squatters) develop. London grew <i>because of</i> industry, while Suva grows <i>without</i> enough industry.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Urban problem in the cartoon: causes and effects on people',a:'e.g. <b>Traffic congestion</b>: too many people and cars for the roads, poor public transport, and everyone travelling to work at the same time. Effects on people: long, stressful journeys, lost work time, more road accidents, and higher costs.\n(or overcrowding and slums: rapid migration and a lack of affordable housing, causing poor sanitation and disease.)'},
{n:'7.',q:'Effect of that problem on the environment',a:'Vehicle exhaust causes <b>air pollution</b> (smog) and greenhouse gases, which harm health and add to climate change. There is also noise pollution, and more land is covered in roads, which increases flooding.'},
{n:'8.',q:'(a) Four urban planning strategies in developed countries (b) effectiveness',a:'(a) 1. <b>Green belts</b> around cities stop urban sprawl (London).\n2. <b>New towns</b> (e.g. Milton Keynes, UK) take people and jobs away from the crowded city.\n3. <b>Better public transport</b>: metro, buses and congestion charging (the London congestion charge, 2003).\n4. <b>Urban renewal / regeneration</b> of old areas (e.g. London Docklands) and zoning laws.\n(b) Green belts protect the countryside <b>but</b> push house prices up. New towns relieved crowding <b>but</b> some became commuter towns. The congestion charge cut traffic by about 15–30% at first <b>but</b> traffic later grew back. Regeneration created jobs and homes <b>but</b> often pushed poor locals out (gentrification). They are effective overall if combined.'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Tourism (20 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Define "tourist amenities"',a:'<b>Facilities and services provided for tourists</b>: hotels, resorts, restaurants, toilets, information centres, tours and entertainment.'},
{n:'2.',q:'One generalisation about world tourism',a:'Most international tourists visit <b>Europe</b> (and North America). Developed regions receive most arrivals, while Africa, the Middle East and the Pacific receive the fewest, although Asia–Pacific is <b>growing fastest</b>.'},
{n:'3.',q:'What causes this pattern (1990–2010)?',a:'Europe has many <b>rich people</b> who take holidays, <b>short distances</b> between countries, open borders (EU), cheap flights, and famous history and culture. Asia grew fast because of rising incomes (e.g. Chinese tourists), cheaper air travel and new resorts. Poor regions have weak infrastructure, conflict and high travel costs.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Two physical factors behind the decline in tourism in 2023',a:'e.g. <b>Extreme weather and natural disasters</b> (heatwaves, wildfires, floods, cyclones, earthquakes) and <b>disease</b> (the after-effects of COVID-19).'},
{n:'5.',q:'Worst-affected regions and how these factors caused the decline',a:'<b>Asia–Pacific</b> (still far below 2019 levels), and parts of the Middle East and Africa. Disasters <b>damage airports, roads and hotels</b> and make places unsafe, so tourists cancel. Disease brought border closures and quarantine, so flights and arrivals stopped and took a long time to recover.'},
{n:'6.',q:'One environmental drawback of mass tourism (Fig. 20)',a:'e.g. <b>Overcrowded beaches and pollution</b>: tourists and hotels produce a lot of <b>rubbish and sewage</b> that pollutes beaches and the sea, harming coral reefs and marine life. Building hotels on the coast destroys dunes and mangroves and causes erosion.'},
{n:'7.',q:'One benefit of tourism to people in the Bahamas',a:'<b>Jobs and income</b>: tourism is the biggest employer (hotels, restaurants, tours, crafts). Local people earn money, and the government earns tax and foreign exchange to spend on schools, roads and health.'},
{n:'8.',q:'(a) Four Pacific tourism management examples (b) evaluate them',a:'(a) 1. <b>Eco-tourism</b> (e.g. eco-lodges in Fiji and Samoa) with small numbers and low impact.\n2. <b>Marine protected areas</b> (e.g. PIPA in Kiribati, Fiji\'s shark reserves), with dive fees paying for conservation.\n3. <b>Village or homestay tourism</b> (Samoan beach fales, Vanuatu bungalows), so the money stays with local families.\n4. <b>Tourism plans and regulations</b>: national tourism offices (e.g. Tourism Authority of Kiribati), environmental impact assessments, visitor fees.\n(b) Eco-tourism and MPAs protect reefs <b>but</b> bring in less money and are hard to police. Homestays spread benefits to villages <b>but</b> quality and marketing can be poor. Regulations reduce damage <b>but</b> enforcement is weak and large resorts are often foreign-owned, so profits leave the country (leakage).'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/GEOGRAPHY EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Model answers based on the MOE marking scheme, rewritten more clearly. Look at the figure in the paper as you read each answer. For case-study questions, use the case studies you learned in class.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Natural hazards (cyclones and volcanoes)', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Jet stream; location and distribution of cyclone source areas',a:'1. A jet stream is a narrow band of very fast wind high in the atmosphere (about 9–16 km up), blowing west to east. Above a cyclone it carries away rising air, so more air can rise and the storm strengthens. E.g. the subtropical jet stream.\n2. Location: over <b>warm tropical oceans</b> between about 5° and 30° N and S, but <b>not on the equator</b> (no Coriolis force there). Distribution: NW Pacific (Philippines–Japan), N Indian Ocean/Bay of Bengal, Caribbean/Gulf of Mexico, S Indian Ocean (Madagascar) and SW Pacific (Fiji–Vanuatu–Queensland). None form in the S Atlantic or SE Pacific (cool water).'},
{n:'3.',q:'Label the volcano',a:'<b>Magma chamber</b> – deep under the volcano. <b>Crater</b> – the opening at the top of the vent. <b>Ash flow/cloud</b> – rising from the crater. <b>Lava flow</b> – running down the side.'},
{n:'4–5.',q:'Factors and process of a tropical cyclone; how they work',a:'4. Factors: sea surface at least <b>26–27 °C</b>; lots of <b>moisture</b> (water vapour); <b>Coriolis force</b> (at least 5° from the equator); low wind shear/jet stream above. Process: <b>evaporation → rising air → condensation</b> (forming a low-pressure system).\n5. The sun heats the ocean strongly in the tropics → warm, moist air rises, leaving <b>low pressure</b> at the surface → air rushes in and also rises → as it rises it <b>cools and condenses</b>, forming huge cumulonimbus clouds and releasing <b>latent heat</b>, which makes the air rise even faster → the Coriolis force makes the inflowing air <b>spin</b> (clockwise in the southern hemisphere) around a calm eye of sinking air. The jet stream removes air at the top, keeping the system going.'},
{n:'6–7.',q:'Mayon 2006 impact on people; a positive effect on the environment',a:'6. In 2006 heavy typhoon rain turned volcanic ash on Mayon into fast <b>mudflows (lahars)</b> that buried villages – about <b>1 266 people were killed</b> because they had no time to escape; homes, farms and roads were destroyed.\n7. Volcanic ash and lava weather into very <b>fertile soils</b> rich in minerals, good for farming. Also: heat near the surface gives <b>geothermal energy</b> and hot springs.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Volcano case study: location, three effects, three strategies',a:'Example – <b>Mt Pinatubo, Philippines (1991)</b>: on Luzon island, about 90 km north-west of Manila, at about 15°N 120°E, on the Pacific Ring of Fire where the Eurasian plate meets the Philippine plate.\nEffects: ash buried farms and towns (about 800 deaths, mostly from collapsing roofs); lahars destroyed homes and bridges for years after; the ash cloud cooled the world by about 0.5 °C and closed Clark Air Base.\nStrategies: <b>monitoring</b> (seismometers, gas, tiltmeters) gave a warning; <b>evacuation</b> of about 60 000 people saved thousands of lives; <b>lahar dykes and channels</b> and hazard maps keep people out of danger zones. Evaluate: monitoring and evacuation were very effective; dykes help but are costly and some failed in big lahars.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Water resources', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Wetland; surplus and deficit areas; Earth\'s water distribution',a:'1. Land that is waterlogged or covered by shallow water for much of the year, e.g. mangrove swamps, marshes, the Mississippi delta swamps.\n2. Surplus: <b>Amazon Basin</b> (South America) or Congo Basin/SE Asia. Deficit: <b>Sahara Desert</b> (N Africa), Middle East or central Australia.\n3. About <b>96.5%</b> of Earth\'s water is salty ocean; only about <b>3.5%</b> is fresh. Most fresh water is locked in <b>ice caps and glaciers</b> or deep <b>groundwater</b>; only a tiny fraction is in lakes, rivers, soil, plants and the air. So very little water is easy for people to use.'},
{n:'4–5.',q:'Deficit: factors and process; surplus: two factors',a:'4. Factors: low and unreliable rainfall/long droughts; high temperatures; little vegetation; impermeable rock or no rivers; overuse by farming. Process: <b>evaporation greater than precipitation</b> (or desertification).\n5. (i) <b>Climate</b> – equatorial areas get heavy convectional rain all year, so rainfall is far more than evaporation. (ii) <b>Relief and vegetation</b> – mountains cause relief rain and feed rivers; dense forest returns moisture by transpiration for more rain; permeable rocks store groundwater.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Israel desert irrigation: positive and negative',a:'Positive: drip <b>irrigation</b> turns desert into productive farmland, raising food supply, exports and incomes.\nNegative: it uses large amounts of scarce water (from aquifers, the Jordan River or desalination) and high evaporation can cause <b>salinisation</b> of soil; neighbouring users get less water.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Three strategies in water-deficit countries; effectiveness',a:'(a) <b>Desalination</b> (Israel, Saudi Arabia, Kiribati\'s small units); <b>drip irrigation and water recycling</b> (Israel reuses about 85% of wastewater); <b>dams/transfer schemes and rainwater harvesting</b> (Kiribati water tanks; Bonriki and Buota water reserves with protected lenses).\n(b) Desalination gives a reliable supply but is expensive and uses a lot of energy. Drip irrigation and recycling greatly reduce waste and are very effective, but cost money to install. Rain tanks and protected groundwater reserves are cheap and suit atolls, but fail in droughts and lenses can be polluted or over-pumped. Overall each works best when combined with conservation.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Population growth', qs:[
{n:'1–2.',q:'Youthful population; factors in population change',a:'1. A population with a large proportion of young people (under 15) – a wide-based pyramid. E.g. Kiribati, Niger.\n2. <b>Natural increase</b> (birth rate − death rate) and <b>net migration</b> (immigration − emigration). The countries on the graph grow at different rates (from under 1% to over 2.5% a year) depending on these.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Physical factor for dense and for sparse population',a:'Dense: <b>flat, fertile river plains and deltas with water</b> (Ganges, Nile, Yangtze) – good for farming and building, so many people live there.\nSparse: <b>extreme climate</b> – hot deserts (Sahara) with no water, or very cold areas (Siberia, Antarctica), and high rugged mountains (Himalayas) where farming and transport are hard.'},
{n:'4–5.',q:'Economic factor for Asia; political factor for Europe',a:'4. Asia has many <b>jobs in manufacturing and industry</b> (China, India) and intensive rice farming that supports many people per km²; factories and cities attract huge numbers of workers.\n5. Europe is politically <b>stable and democratic</b>; the <b>European Union</b> lets people move freely and protects human rights, so people stay and migrants are attracted there.'},
{n:'6–7.',q:'Two issues in the cartoon; how population change causes them',a:'6. <b>Climate change/global warming</b> (melting ice, polar bear losing its habitat) and continued <b>greenhouse gas emissions</b> (pollution).\n7. More people need more energy, transport and food. Burning coal, oil and gas releases CO₂; more livestock and landfill waste release methane; forests are cleared for farms and cities. These gases trap heat, warming the planet and melting polar ice.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Strategies for a low-growth country; effectiveness',a:'Example – <b>Japan</b> or <b>Singapore</b> (also France, Sweden).\n(a) <b>Baby bonuses and tax breaks</b> for families (Singapore Baby Bonus); <b>paid parental leave and cheap childcare</b> (France, Sweden); <b>immigration of workers</b> to fill labour shortages (Japan\'s new visas, Germany).\n(b) Money incentives have had only a small effect – Singapore\'s fertility is still about 1.0. Childcare and leave work better (France about 1.8) but are costly. Immigration quickly fills jobs but can cause social tension. Overall growth is hard to raise; countries combine all three.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Urbanisation', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Low-density residential; a pattern; why it exists',a:'1. Housing zone with large sections and houses spread out, usually in the suburbs, e.g. outer suburbs of Auckland or Sydney.\n2. In developed regions urbanisation is already high and rising <b>slowly</b> (e.g. about 30% → over 80%); in less developed regions it is rising <b>rapidly</b> (South America about 42% in 1950 to about 89% by 2050).\n3. Developed countries urbanised during the industrial revolution and have low birth rates, and some people move out to the suburbs and countryside. Developing countries have high birth rates in cities and strong <b>rural–urban migration</b> (push: poverty, few services; pull: jobs, schools, hospitals).'},
{n:'4–5.',q:'Causes in Fig 15 (industrial) and Fig 16 (migration); differences',a:'4. Fig 15: the <b>Industrial Revolution</b> (factory jobs). Fig 16: <b>rural–urban migration</b> caused by push–pull factors.\n5. (i) <b>Time</b>: industrial urbanisation happened in the 1750s–1900s in Europe and N America; migration-led urbanisation mainly since the 1950s in developing countries. (ii) <b>Reason</b>: in Fig 15 people were pulled by plentiful factory jobs; in Fig 16 people are mostly pushed by rural poverty and lack of services, and many cities don\'t have enough jobs, leading to slums.'},
{n:'6–7.',q:'Urban model; impacts on people and environment',a:'6. <b>Multiple-nuclei model</b>: there is more than one centre (CBD plus outlying business districts), and similar land uses (housing, industry) appear in several separate zones.\n7. As cars and roads spread, wealthier people move to suburbs and new business centres grow there; poorer people stay near industry, so the city becomes divided by income. Cities sprawl outward, replacing farmland and natural habitats, increasing traffic, air pollution and the cost of services.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four urban planning strategies in developing countries; effectiveness',a:'(a) <b>Site-and-service schemes</b> and low-cost housing (e.g. Nairobi, Lima); <b>slum upgrading</b> (water, toilets and paths in existing settlements – Rio favelas, Kiribati\'s Betio water/sanitation projects); <b>new towns/decentralisation</b> to move growth away from the capital (Brasília, Kiribati outer-island development); <b>better public transport</b> (Curitiba bus rapid transit).\n(b) Site-and-service and upgrading are cheap and help many people, but can\'t keep up with fast growth. New towns reduce pressure but are costly and slow to attract people. BRT in Curitiba is very effective at cutting congestion. Overall they help, but only if funding and planning keep pace with population growth.'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Tourism', qs:[
{n:'1–3.',q:'Origin/source; Africa\'s arrivals and receipts; distribution pattern',a:'1. The country a tourist comes from, e.g. most tourists to Fiji come from Australia and New Zealand.\n2. Africa received about <b>45 million</b> international tourists, spending about <b>US$17.2 billion</b> on hotels, food and transport – a small share of the world total but an important income.\n3. Most tourists both come from and go to rich regions – <b>Europe</b> receives by far the most (about 584 million), then the Americas and Asia-Pacific; the Middle East and Africa receive the fewest. Developing regions are growing as destinations.'},
{n:'4–5.',q:'Thailand: factors 2019–21; a social-cultural factor 2013–19',a:'4. Arrivals collapsed from 2019 to 2021 because of the <b>COVID-19 pandemic</b>: border closures/lockdowns and the halt of international flights.\n5. Thailand\'s <b>rich culture</b> – temples, festivals, food, friendly people – promoted through advertising and social media, plus more leisure time and paid holidays in source countries, attracted rising numbers.'},
{n:'6–7.',q:'Social-cultural drawback; environmental impact',a:'6. Tourists\' <b>dress and behaviour</b> (revealing clothes, drinking) can offend local customs; young locals may copy them, weakening traditional culture.\n7. <b>Litter and plastic pollution</b>: bottles, cups and packaging left by tourists wash into the sea, harming marine life and spoiling beaches because plastic does not break down.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Four tourism management strategies; effectiveness',a:'(a) <b>Ecotourism</b> (Costa Rica, Fiji village stays); <b>marine protected areas and park fees</b> (Phoenix Islands Protected Area, Kiribati; Galápagos); <b>limits on numbers/zoning</b> (Bhutan\'s high daily fee, Maya Bay closure in Thailand); <b>training and employing locals / cultural codes of conduct</b>.\n(b) Ecotourism brings income to communities and protects nature, but it is small-scale. Protected areas and fees fund conservation and keep reefs healthy, though enforcement is hard in remote areas. Limits on numbers reduce damage (Maya Bay recovered) but cut income. Local employment keeps money in the country and respects culture. Overall these work best together.'}
]}
]}
]});
